FINAL ENTERPRISE CLEANUP & NORMALIZATION PATCH
Document Governance
This Information Architecture document is governed under the VyaparNet PRDv2 Change Control Policy.

All future changes must pass:
- workflow impact analysis
- database impact review
- operational review
- UX consistency validation
- scalability review

No uncontrolled IA-level changes are permitted after freeze approval.
Role Isolation Principle
Every role experiences a purpose-built interface.

Buyers:
- discovery-first

Suppliers:
- operations-first

Operators:
- task-first

Admins:
- control-first

No role should experience unnecessary interface complexity from another role's workflow.
Human Fallback Experience
If any automated workflow fails:
- buyer can escalate to WhatsApp support,
- operator intervention becomes available,
- admin override supported,
- manual procurement possible.

Platform reliability must never depend solely on automation success.
Supplier Trust Philosophy
Suppliers are operational partners, not passive vendors.

The platform must:
- clearly explain penalties,
- reward reliability,
- provide transparent score movement,
- avoid hidden operational punishments,
- maintain dispute visibility.
Field Operation Principles
Operator workflows are optimized for:
- fast execution,
- weak network environments,
- low device capability,
- camera-first evidence collection,
- minimal typing,
- rapid escalation.

Operator workflows prioritize speed and proof collection over visual complexity.
Exception Response Principle
Every critical exception must:
- have an owner,
- have a visible priority,
- have an escalation timer,
- appear in operational dashboards,
- generate an audit trail,
- remain unresolved until explicitly closed.
Notification Fatigue Control
The platform must avoid excessive notification spam.

Rules:
- merge duplicate notifications,
- prioritize action-required alerts,
- suppress unnecessary informational spam,
- allow notification preference management later.
Draft Recovery Guarantee
User-generated work should never be silently lost.

Draft recovery applies to:
- order composition,
- quotation requests,
- QC submissions,
- dispatch uploads,
- admin approvals,
- dispute resolutions.
Segment Expansion Principle
New business segments must be addable without:
- platform rewrite,
- database redesign,
- navigation restructuring,
- search engine replacement,
- frontend rebuild.

All segment systems must remain configuration-driven wherever possible.
Non-Goals (MVP)
The MVP intentionally excludes:
- native mobile applications,
- microservices architecture,
- OpenSearch infrastructure,
- advanced AI automation,
- fintech lending systems,
- self-serve marketplace onboarding,
- autonomous procurement AI,
- advanced recommendation engines,
- social-commerce systems,
- supplier advertisement systems.

These remain future architecture placeholders only.

VyaparNet Information Architecture — FINAL OFFICIAL FREEZE

Document Version: VyaparNet_IA_Final_Official_Freeze_v1
Status: LOCKED
Architecture State: FINAL FREEZE APPROVED
Governed By: VyaparNet PRDv2 Change Control Policy

Purpose:
This document is the single authoritative Information Architecture blueprint for VyaparNet.
It combines:
- detailed workflow architecture
- operational governance architecture
- Bharat-first UX principles
- enterprise workflow systems
- offline-first systems
- escalation architecture
- notification systems
- segment isolation principles
- future scalability principles

1. Information Architecture Philosophy
Information Architecture defines:
- navigation structure
- role boundaries
- workflow sequencing
- operational visibility
- information relationships
- system interaction flow

IA is finalized before:
- UI design
- wireframes
- development
- API implementation

The platform is workflow-first, not screen-first.
2. Bharat-First UX Principles
Core principles:
- low cognitive load
- mobile-first design
- progressive disclosure
- WhatsApp fallback
- low-bandwidth optimization
- multilingual-ready architecture
- trust visibility
- rapid operational flow
- minimal typing
- segment isolation

The platform must remain:
- simple for small buyers
- operationally efficient for suppliers
- scalable for enterprise operations
3. Global Navigation Architecture
Every role experiences a purpose-built interface.

Buyer:
- discovery-first

Supplier:
- operations-first

Operator:
- task-first

Admin:
- governance-first

No role should experience unnecessary complexity from another role's workflows.
4. Segment Isolation Architecture
Textile and Spare Parts operate as isolated business environments.

Isolation applies to:
- categories
- search
- filters
- recommendations
- analytics
- dashboards
- workflows
- notifications

Future segments:
- pharma
- electronics
- machinery
must follow same architecture.

Cross-segment visibility prohibited unless explicitly authorized.
5. Buyer Information Architecture
Buyer workflows include:
- onboarding
- business profile setup
- segment selection
- category browsing
- dynamic search
- product discovery
- quotation requests
- saved lists
- sourcing requests
- order placement
- order tracking
- reorder flow
- support escalation

Buyer priorities:
- simplicity
- procurement speed
- trust visibility
- transparent pricing
- easy discovery
6. Supplier Information Architecture
Supplier workflows include:
- stock updates
- order confirmation
- pricing updates
- dispatch proof uploads
- invoice uploads
- catalog management
- payment visibility
- dispute handling
- fulfillment tracking
- scorecard visibility

Supplier score system:
- transparent
- explainable
- audit-visible

Examples:
- late dispatch penalties
- QC penalties
- reliability bonuses
- fulfillment bonuses
7. Operator Information Architecture
Operator workflows include:
- stock verification
- QC verification
- dispatch proof uploads
- field visit notes
- supplier confirmations
- escalation handling
- evidence collection

Operator architecture priorities:
- offline-first execution
- weak-network resilience
- camera-first workflows
- minimal typing
- rapid sync recovery
- fast field execution
8. Admin Information Architecture
Admin systems include:
- operational overview
- procurement management
- supplier governance
- buyer governance
- catalog governance
- payment management
- audit systems
- dispute handling
- exception center
- reporting systems
- feature flag management

Admin dashboards prioritize:
- governance
- operational visibility
- auditability
- exception awareness
- scalability oversight
9. Search & Discovery Architecture
Search systems support:
- segment-specific discovery
- independent filters
- recent searches
- saved searches
- trending searches
- dynamic attributes
- no-result fallback flows
- sourcing requests

No-result flows:
- request sourcing
- upload image
- contact support

Search behavior always remains segment-aware.
10. Notification Architecture
Supported channels:
- in-app notifications
- WhatsApp
- SMS fallback
- email summaries
- future push notifications

Priority levels:
- critical
- high
- normal
- low

Notification systems must:
- avoid spam
- merge duplicates
- prioritize actionable alerts
- support escalation
11. Exception Management Architecture
Centralized Exception Center manages:
- delayed orders
- procurement failures
- QC failures
- fraud alerts
- supplier SLA breaches
- payment mismatches
- stock mismatches

Every exception:
- has an owner
- has priority visibility
- supports escalation
- generates audit logs
- remains visible until resolved
12. Offline-First Operational Design
Offline support required for:
- QC uploads
- dispatch proof uploads
- stock confirmations
- operator notes
- field evidence uploads

System behavior:
- local queue storage
- reconnect sync
- conflict resolution
- upload retry systems
- offline recovery support
13. Draft State & Session Recovery
Draft support required for:
- order creation
- quotations
- dispatch uploads
- QC submissions
- dispute handling
- admin approvals

Session recovery principles:
- restore unsaved work
- restore last context
- prevent silent data loss
- support interrupted workflows
14. Dynamic Taxonomy Architecture
Every segment supports:
- independent categories
- custom attributes
- custom filters
- custom metadata
- independent search logic

New segments must be addable without:
- platform redesign
- DB restructuring
- frontend rewrite
- search replacement
15. Operational Ownership & Auditability
Every workflow entity maintains:
- ownership visibility
- assignment history
- escalation visibility
- activity timeline
- audit traceability

Ownership changes require:
- timestamp
- actor identity
- change reason
16. Human Escalation & Fallback Systems
If automation fails:
- WhatsApp escalation available
- operator intervention supported
- admin override supported
- manual workflows allowed

Platform reliability must never depend solely on automation.
17. Loading, Empty & Recovery States
The platform supports:
- skeleton loaders
- low-bandwidth placeholders
- guided empty states
- workflow recovery states

Examples:
- No orders yet
- No saved products
- No search results

Every empty state must guide the user toward the next action.
18. Saved Lists & Procurement Collections
Buyers can:
- save products
- create procurement collections
- build seasonal lists
- maintain reorder groups

Examples:
- Summer Collection
- Festival Sarees
- Activa Consumables
19. Business Intelligence Placeholders
Future-ready placeholders:
- fast-moving products
- regional demand trends
- price drop alerts
- low-stock alerts
- festival demand signals

These remain placeholders in MVP.
20. Help & Education Systems
Platform supports:
- onboarding guidance
- contextual tooltips
- workflow education
- ordering help
- multilingual assistance
- WhatsApp support

Goal:
reduce onboarding friction for Bharat-first users.
21. Universal Inbox Placeholder
Future architecture placeholder:
- unified support inbox
- WhatsApp integration
- sourcing requests
- dispute communication
- operational escalations

Not part of MVP implementation.
22. Feature Flag Architecture
Feature flags support:
- controlled rollout
- operational testing
- beta features
- safe deployment

Initial implementation:
- database-backed flags
- admin toggles
- environment-aware
23. Operational Principles
Platform philosophy:
- workflow-first
- operationally transparent
- audit-driven
- scalable
- configuration-oriented
- future-extensible

Every workflow must prioritize:
- clarity
- traceability
- operational reliability
24. MVP Constraints & Non-Goals
MVP intentionally excludes:
- native mobile apps
- microservices architecture
- OpenSearch infrastructure
- advanced AI systems
- fintech lending
- advanced recommendation engines
- supplier advertisements
- autonomous procurement AI
- self-serve marketplace onboarding

These remain future placeholders only.
